{"key": 40, "source": "MMMU/MMMU validation (HuggingFace, Apache-2.0)", "source_id": "validation_Diagnostics_and_Laboratory_Medicine_23", "subject": "Diagnostics_and_Laboratory_Medicine", "subfield": "Neuropathology", "answer": "A", "question_type": "multiple-choice", "topic_difficulty": "Medium", "img_type": ["Microscopic Images", "Pathological Images"], "image_file": "mmmu_validation_Diagnostics_and_Laboratory_Medicine_23.png", "image_bytes": 27566, "options_count": 4, "explanation": "The lesion shows numerous eosinophils, multinucleated giant cells, histiocytes and Langerhans cells (which are identified by their folded, 'coffee bean-like' nucleus). These findings are characteristic of Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis (LCH). One type of LCH is eosinophilic granuloma. Osteosarcoma is rare in the skull and would have a high mitotic rate. A malignant germ cell tumor would not have eosinophils and do not occur in the skull. Trauma with repair may have rare eosinophils, but would lack Langerhans cells (which can be identified using monoclonal antibody CD1a)"}
